<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Vistulans</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 990 CE (approx.) · capital Krakow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A West Slavic tribe of the upper Vistula, centred on Krakow, whose pagan prince was warned by Saint Methodius to accept baptism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Vistulans settled the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> basin in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with their centre on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel Hill</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> and another stronghold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wislica">Wislica</a>. They are named in the 9th-century list known as the Bavarian Geographer and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-methodius">Life of Saint Methodius</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their territory was a fertile, densely settled region on trade routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-moravia">Moravia</a> and Rus', and the rich hill fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> suggests a wealthy ruling elite. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-methodius">Life of Methodius</a> tells of a powerful pagan prince on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> who persecuted Christians and was urged to be baptised willingly before he was baptised by force in a foreign land.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The prophecy seems to have come true when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svatopluk-i-of-moravia">Svatopluk</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-moravia">Great Moravia</a> brought the Vistulans under his rule around 880. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> collapsed, the region passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-bohemia">Duchy of Bohemia</a>, and around 990 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-poland">Duchy of Poland</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a>, which later became the Polish royal capital.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vistulans.jpg" alt="Vistulans" width="320" height="241"><figcaption>Image: Tankred · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_europe_9th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 990 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Krakow</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Early Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wislanie, Vislane</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=895&amp;focus=vistulans" aria-label="Vistulans on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 436 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vistulans lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 692L5417 706M5457 692L5330 692M5457 692L5395 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5417 706h0M5330 692h0M5395 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Krakow. <a href="/atlas?year=895&amp;focus=vistulans">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
